WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.910
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.360
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.390
Close enough!

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:18.680
As you have probably noticed, today we are
going to talk about text to speech, or TTS

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.680
in short.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.669
TTS means that we write a piece of text, and
a computer synthesized voice will read it

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:25.869
aloud for us.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:30.889
This is really useful for reading the news,
or creating audiobooks that don't have any

00:00:30.889 --> 00:00:32.680
official voiceovers.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.559
This work was done by researchers at Princeton
University and Adobe and is about text-based

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:40.600
audio narration editing.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.820
This one is going to be crazy good.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.960
The Adobe guys like to call this the Photoshop
of voiceovers.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.730
In a normal situation, we have access to a
waveform and if we wish to change anything

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.010
in a voiceover, we need to edit it.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:00.210
Editing waveforms by hand is extremely difficult
- traditional techniques often can't even

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.439
reliably find the boundaries between words
or letters, let alone edit them.

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:11.140
And with this technique, we can cut, copy,
and even edit this text and the waveforms

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:30.399
will automatically be transformed appropriately
using the same voice.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:02:05.930
We can even use new words that have never
been uttered in the original narration.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:11.500
It solves an optimization problem where the
similarity, smoothness and the pace of the

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.450
original footage is to be matched as closely
as possible.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.060
One of the excellent new features is that
we can even choose from several different

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.830
voicings for the new word and insert the one
that we deem the most appropriate.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.780
For expert users, the pitch and duration is
also editable.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.970
It is always important to have a look at a
new technique and make sure that it works

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.590
well in practice, but in science, this is
only the first step.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:43.300
There has to be more proof that a new proposed
method works well in a variety of cases.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:48.220
In this case, a theoretical proof by means
of mathematics is not feasible, therefore

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:54.940
a user study was carried out where listeners
were shown synthesized and real audio samples

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.950
and had to blindly decide which was which.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.320
The algorithm was remarkably successful at
deceiving the test subjects.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.650
Make sure to have a look at the paper in the
description for more details.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.570
This technique is traditional in a sense that
it doesn't use any sort of neural networks,

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:15.950
however, there are great strides being made
in that area as well, which I am quite excited

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.880
to show you in future episodes.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.950
And due to some of these newer video and audio
editing techniques, I expect that within the

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.060
internet forums, fake news is going to be
an enduring topic.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.570
I hope that in parallel with better and better
text and video synthesis, there will be an

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:37.350
arms race with other methods that are designed
to identify these cases.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.760
A neural detective, if you will.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.220
And now, if you excuse me, I'll give this
publicly available TTS one more try and see

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.260
if I can retire from narrating videos.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:53.900
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.000
Yep.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.540
Exact same thing.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.800
Bet you didn't even notice it.

